<blockquote>
<p>hovers at altitudes between 1.5 and 2860 meters above sea level (dependent on the changes in distance from the Earth to the Sun between summer and winter.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If I'm completely misunderstanding what you mean here let me know.</p>
<p>First, the Earth is closest to the sun in January and farthest in July. It has nothing to do with winter and summer. In January, this object would be in the northern hemisphere (where it's winter) and in July, it would be in the southern hemisphere (where it's winter). And in between, it would get closer to the equator.</p>
<p>Second, you write that it's observable in India in the winter, so&#8230; The sphere gets closer to the Earth as the Earth gets closer to the sun? Is that your intent? Because the opposite makes a little more sense to me&#8230; But then you'd be seeing it close to Earth in South America, southern Africa, and Australia, instead of Mexico and India. Today. These change over time, but I'm not sure whether they change relative to the axial tilt, and if so over what timescale. The point is, if the distance between this object and the Earth varies related to the distance between the sun and the Earth, spell out which way it's related, and maybe double check things were the same 4500 years ago&#8230;</p>
<p>Third, the distance between earth and the sun varies by 5 million kilometres between January and July, not 2500 metres. Just putting that out there, like I said you didn't spell out the relationship.</p>
<p>If there's a gap in my understanding of this article (or of Lagrange points which I think I understand and Earth's orbital parameters which I literally just googled) lemme know.</p>

