			<div class="content" id="post-content-3586362">
									

<p>Also, for fun&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>On the average, one revolution of the Earth relative to perihelion is about 25 minutes longer than one revolution relative to the December solstice. Perihelion advances one full calendar date every 60 or so years.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>During the late Mexica period, it would have fallen a few days after solstice, placing the sphere clearly in that area.</p>
<p>In the 1st or 2nd century BC, it would have fallen in the first few days of December, probably right near where you have it in India.</p>
<p>However, 4500 years ago, perihelion would have fallen about 61 days before December solstice (equivalent of October 21st if there had been such a thing as October back then). Which would have put the sphere more around&#8230; 10.5-11° N according to a calculator I found.</p>
<p>South Sudan, or Nicaragua, or Vietnam&#8230; I'm not sure, any place that officially had antechambers or hieroglyphics. Unless that monument was somewhere over 400m tall (I dunno exactly) it wasn't getting knocked off&#8230; Unless you handwave it as &quot;hieroglyphics and antechambers the public doesn't know about&quot;, in which case they could also be anomalously tall :p</p>
<p>Interestingly (to me at least) the sidereal year (revolution around the sun relative to fixed stars) is about 21 minutes longer than the tropical year (time between solstices/equinoxes) and about 4 minutes shorter than the anomalistic year (time between perihelions/aphelions).</p>

