					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/doctor-sykes" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1820353); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1820353&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607028003" alt="Doctor_Sykes" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1820353)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/doctor-sykes" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1820353); return false;" >Doctor_Sykes</a></span> <span class="odate time_1505092606 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Sep 2017 01:16</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3589275">
									

<p>So, after reading through this, I definitely like the idea of an &quot;unstoppable object&quot; with a fixed position relative to another object, and the idea of having it follow the sun was also interesting. However, the last few addenda, including the ending &quot;explanation,&quot; seem a bit forced and unrealistically wacky, (like the article goes from an in-depth explanation of the trajectory of the object to a short paragraph about a guy who apparently invented infinite power somehow) which disturbs the tone of the article.</p>
<p>The fact that only a few ancient people knew about it, especially when it gets as close as 1.5 meters to the ground, also suspends disbelief a little, and there's no anomalous explanation for that. Plus, as others have mentioned, the fact that the object is a tiny replica of Earth seems a little arbitrary. In any case, while it's clear you put a lot of thought into the science of the object, the attempts at contextualizing the object in history and explaining its origins, as well as the fact that it is basically uncontainable and yet is not very often seen ruined it a bit for me.</p>
<p>I want to up-vote for such a creative idea, but the execution has forced me to a no-vote (it might just be my tastes but still).</p>

							</div>

								
					
